;;; Multipattern e-matching, hand-encoded on top of the slotted machinery.
;;;
;;; THE ENCODING, one shape of user rule per section: the problem the shape poses, the
;;; construction that solves it, and then the rule -- the one a compiler emits, one per
;;; section and nothing else.  Read it top to bottom.  `slotted-user-rules.md` is the prose
;;; version, and `slotted-experiments/slotted-encoder.py` is this same recipe as code --
;;; one implementation, which every generator goes through: `xdiff.py` (the toy language,
;;; checked against the reference), `xarray.py` (the paper's array language, likewise)
;;; and `gen-sdql-rules.py` (SDQL).
;;;
;;; EVERY RULE BELOW IS A RULE.  Six of the eleven are a rewrite someone actually wrote --
;;; four from `sdql_rules()` in `slotted-egraphs/benches/sdql.rs`, two from the paper's S4.1
;;; array language (`tests/array/mod.rs`, whose guard polarity `tests/rise/rewrite.rs` states
;;; correctly) -- and each section names which.  The other five shapes have no real instance:
;;; three of them need a MULTIPATTERN, which no single-rooted rewrite produces, and two need
;;; a REDUNDANT SLOT, which comes from a merge and not from a rule's shape.  Those sections
;;; say so and say why.  No section concludes into a witness relation: the conclusion is the
;;; `Equated` the encoder emits, so what the tests observe is what the rule DOES.  There is
;;; no `relation` declaration in this file.
;;;
;;; All eleven are the encoder's own output, variable names, line breaks and comments aside,
;;; and for the six real ones it is the SAME output as the committed generated files: M9 and
;;; M11 match `eta` and `let-app` in `tests/slotted-array-rules.egg` exactly, and M1, M2, M4
;;; and M10 match `eq-comm`, `sub-identity`, `mult-assoc1` and `add-zero` in
;;; `tests/slotted-sdql-rules.egg` up to the constructor spelling -- that file gives sdql its
;;; own `Equality`/`Sub`/`Mult`/`Add` constructors, where this one uses the generic
;;; `(App2 "eq" ...)` with the operator in a payload column.  Nothing about the recipe
;;; depends on which.
;;;
;;; THE REFERENCE, wherever one is mentioned below, is `memoryleak47/slotted-egraphs` --
;;; the Rust implementation from the PLDI 2025 paper -- on its PR #45.  The multipattern
;;; e-matcher (`src/rewrite/multipat.rs`) is already in that crate's `main`; what #45 adds
;;; is a canonicalisation fix in `extend_subst` plus a multipattern test suite, and it is
;;; the branch this encoding is differentially tested against.
;;;
;;; No terms, no schedules, no assertions, no wrong versions.
;;; `tests/slotted-user-rules-tests.egg` includes this file and supplies all of those, so
;;; the rules below are the ones under test rather than a copy of them, and it is also where
;;; the counter-examples live -- for each construction, the spelling it rules out together
;;; with the e-graph that tells the two apart.
;;;
;;; OPERATOR NAMES.  Every rule below is live at once, so a term built for one section can
;;; in principle match another section's rule.  A real rule brings the real operator, so
;;; the overlaps are the ones the source languages have: `app` is in M9 and M11, and
;;; `(Num 0)` is written by M2 and read by M10.  `eq`, `*`, `-`, `+`, `lambda` and `let`
;;; each occur in one section only, so what has to hold is that M9's terms contain no `let`
;;; and M11's no `lambda`, and that neither M2's nor M10's terms contain the other's
;;; operator -- none of them do.  Checked by flipping every assertion in the tests file, not
;;; assumed.  The five synthetic sections take the reference's own multipattern operators
;;; (`f`, `g`, `h`, `k` in `tests/multipat/mod.rs`) with the section number appended --
;;; `f3`, `g5`, `h7` -- since their shapes are alike and all five are live together.  A
;;; counter-example over in the tests file takes a further letter where it would otherwise
;;; collide with the rule it is contrasted with -- `-a`, `f3a`, `f3b`, `*a`, `f5a`, `f6a`,
;;; `f6b`, `f8a`, `+a` -- so that rule cannot mask it.
;;;
;;; Conventions:
;;;   (compose a b)           = a . b, b first, and TRUNCATES: keys b sends outside dom(a)
;;;                             are dropped
;;;   (compose-total a b)     = the same, but fails rather than dropping a key
;;;   (map-image m)           = identity on im(m);  (map-domain m) = identity on dom(m)
;;;   (find-mapping-total avoid domain first... second...)
;;;                           = least injective m with m . second_i = first_i, made total on
;;;                             the keys of `domain`, minting outside `avoid`'s slots (M3)
;;;   (App2 f m1 c1 m2 c2)    = f(m1*c1, m2*c2),  mi : slots(ci) -> slots(node)
;;;   (App3 f m1 c1 m2 c2 m3 c3)  the same at arity 3; `let` is the one used here
;;;   (Num n), (Sym s)        payload leaves, depending on no slots
;;;   (RenamesToLeader a m b) = a = m*b, m : slots(b) -> slots(a); a self-loop is a member
;;;                             of G(a)
;;;   (Equated a m b)         = the same fact with no orientation picked -- what an ACTION
;;;                             writes; the machinery derives the oriented row (M10)
;;;   (ClassSlots c)          = identity on the slots c depends on; only ever narrows (M8)
;;;
;;; Scaffolding names, the same in every section:
;;;   pN, qN, rN  atom N's own stored edges, one per child column
;;;   domN        identity on atom N's node's slots (the union of its edge images)
;;;   mpN         atom N's renaming, taking its node's slots into pattern slots
;;;   imN         identity on the slots mpN named;  avoidN accumulates those up to atom N
;;;   mX          variable X's renaming into slots(pattern), as read off the node
;;;   csX         (ClassSlots X); `(compose mX csX)` is X's renaming narrowed to its own
;;;               class, which is the form every use takes (M8)
;;;   symX        a self-loop of X, i.e. a symmetry
;;;   sX          the pattern slot a slot literal `$X` was pinned to
;;;
;;; Contents, in order.  Each section names its rule, the language it comes from and the
;;; harness cases covering it, and `slotted-user-rules.md` refers to these labels.
;;;   M1   one atom, nothing shared             sdql   eq-comm
;;;   M2   a repeated variable, Def. 6           sdql   sub-identity
;;;   M3   why an atom's renaming is total       --     synthetic: a multipattern
;;;   M4   two atoms, chained                    sdql   mult-assoc1
;;;   M5   the avoid-set, and why it accumulates  --    synthetic: two redundant slots
;;;   M6   two atoms, joined on a fresh root      --    synthetic: a multipattern
;;;   M7   three atoms, the cases compose         --    synthetic: a multipattern
;;;   M8   a variable wider than its class        --    synthetic: a redundant slot
;;;   M9   a slot literal                        array  eta
;;;   M10  actions: concluding over renamed ids   sdql   add-zero
;;;   M11  a nested right-hand side              array  let-app
;;;
;;; Every rule below assumes the phased schedule the tests run: the invariant rules are
;;; saturated between user-rule steps, so a rule here only ever matches nodes that are
;;; already canonical.
;;;
;;;     (run-schedule (saturate (run slotted))
;;;                   (repeat N (seq (run) (saturate (run slotted)))))

(include "tests/slotted-node-rules.egg")


;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
;;; M1.  One atom, nothing shared.
;;;
;;;     sdql's eq-comm:   (eq ?a ?b)  =>  (eq ?b ?a)
;;;
;;; The LHS is flattened into depth-1 atoms `?v == (Op ?c1 ?c2)`, one per e-node with every
;;; child a bare variable, and each becomes one egglog atom, so egglog's join does the
;;; e-class level for free.  With a single atom nothing is known before it: its node's slots
;;; ARE the pattern's slots and the stored edges already are the children's renamings into
;;; them, so there is nothing to constrain.  `mp1` is the identity on `dom1`, and what is
;;; left is three `ClassSlots` lookups -- no `find-mapping-total`, no group enumerated.
;;;
;;; Covered by: M1-commutativity.
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

(rule ((= e (App2 "eq" p1 a q1 b))
       (= dom1 (map-union (map-image p1) (map-image q1)))
       (= mp1 dom1)                            ; atom 1: the identity on its node's slots
       (= avoid1 (map-image mp1))              ; and those slots ARE slots(pattern)
       (= ma (compose mp1 p1))
       (= csa (ClassSlots a))
       (= mb (compose mp1 q1))
       (= csb (ClassSlots b))
       (= cse (ClassSlots e)))
      ((let rhs (App2 "eq" (compose mb csb) b (compose ma csa) a))
       (Equated rhs (compose mp1 cse) e)))


;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
;;; M2.  One atom, a repeated variable -- the Def. 6 check.
;;;
;;;     sdql's sub-identity:   (- ?e ?e)  =>  0
;;;
;;; What the join does NOT give is agreement of the renamings: egglog's variable equality
;;; is e-class equality, while slotted equality is equality of renamed ids.  Supplying that
;;; difference is what every section here is about, and this is its smallest instance.
;;;
;;; A variable `x` compiles to two egglog variables -- its leader `x` and a renaming
;;; `mx : slots(x) -> slots(pattern)` -- standing for the paper's `beta(x) = mx * x`.  Here
;;; the two occurrences are `p1*x` and `q1*x`, and Def. 6 says they agree iff `p1^-1 . q1`
;;; is in G(x).  The rule states exactly that: `symx` ranges over the self-loops of `x`, and
;;; the equation asks for one carrying the second edge onto the first.  Both edges come off
;;; the matched row, so at most one element can satisfy it -- the join is a lookup, not a
;;; search, which is what makes this the cheap case; M6 is where a group really is
;;; enumerated.
;;;
;;; Demanding instead that the two edges be *equal* -- what a non-slotted encoding would
;;; write -- is a strictly STRONGER condition than Def. 6, since `p1 = q1` makes the group
;;; element the identity, which is in every G(x).  So that rule is incomplete: it misses
;;; matches Def. 6 admits, and a symmetric child is where the two part ways.  `-a` in the
;;; tests file is that rule.
;;;
;;; The conclusion is the paper's own: `(- ?e ?e)` is the literal `0`, which depends on no
;;; slot, so the row says the matched class's slots are all redundant.
;;;
;;; Covered by: C1, C4, S1.
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

(rule ((= e (App2 "-" p1 x q1 x))
       (= dom1 (map-union (map-image p1) (map-image q1)))
       (= mp1 dom1)
       (= avoid1 (map-image mp1))
       (= mx (compose mp1 p1))
       (= csx (ClassSlots x))
       ;; Def. 6 for the second occurrence: some symmetry of `x` carries one edge to the
       ;; other.  Determined, since both edges come off the matched row.
       (RenamesToLeader x symx x)
       (= (compose mp1 q1) (compose (compose mx csx) symx))
       (= cse (ClassSlots e)))
      ((Equated (Num 0) (compose mp1 cse) e)))


;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
;;; M3.  Why an atom's renaming has to be TOTAL.
;;;
;;;     ?p == (f3 ?x ?y),  ?q == (g3 ?x ?z)   =>   ?p = (h3 ?y ?z)
;;;
;;; SYNTHETIC, and this is the shape that has to be.  Atom 2's root `?q` is a variable no
;;; earlier atom bound, which only a MULTIPATTERN can produce: a rewrite's left-hand side is
;;; a single-rooted tree, so every atom but the first is a child of an earlier one and has
;;; its root already bound.  None of the three corpora -- the array language, rise, sdql --
;;; has a multi-rooted rule, and the reference's own multipattern suite is synthetic for the
;;; same reason: `tests/multipat/mod.rs` declares a language of `f`, `g`, `h`, `k`, `p`, `q`
;;; for exactly this purpose, and the operators here are its, numbered.
;;;
;;; The smallest shape whose renamings are not determined, and the reason every atom from M4
;;; on is solved with `find-mapping-total`.  Only `?x` is shared, so atom 2's renaming is
;;; pinned on im(p2) and nowhere else: a slot of `?q` outside it gets NO name in
;;; slots(pattern).  S3.6 asks for the *minimal* renaming carrying the node into pattern
;;; slots, and a minimal one simply omits that slot.
;;;
;;; An EDGE may not omit a slot.  Def. 5 makes `dom(m) = slots(a)` the leader condition on a
;;; renamed id `m*a`, and Def. 7 requires every renamed id inside a shape to be a leader, so
;;; a stored edge's domain is exactly its child's slot set.  A dropped slot leaves a row
;;; asserting the child does not depend on a slot it has -- unsound, and the tests file's
;;; counter-example (a) is that rule.
;;;
;;; What licenses the fix is Def. 8, which asks only for SOME extension `m''` of `m'` and
;;; says so: it "allows an arbitrary renaming for the redundant slots".  Any total extension
;;; over the unpinned slots therefore represents the same e-nodes.  S3.6 says it
;;; operationally in its closing line -- if a class "contains an e-node with a redundant or
;;; bound slot ... it suffices to pick any fresh slot for them" -- and the reference does
;;; exactly that in `enodes_applied`, freshening every slot outside the class's slot set
;;; before it matches.  `find-mapping-total` is that: `find-mapping`'s constraints and
;;; injectivity check, plus a minted name for every domain slot they leave unnamed.
;;; `domain`'s keys are the slots the result must cover, `avoid`'s are the ones it may not
;;; mint over, and both are identity maps the caller already has.  This is the spelling
;;; every section below uses.
;;;
;;; Declining to fire instead -- `compose-total` in place of `compose` -- is sound but
;;; incomplete; that is counter-example (b).  Note that `?p` losing a slot is NOT the
;;; symptom: `f3(?x,?y) = h3(?y,?z)` genuinely says `f3` does not depend on `?x`, so the
;;; redundancy is derived by the fixed rule too.
;;;
;;; The redundant-slot half of the same story is real, and M4's counter-example is it: in a
;;; CHAIN the constraint pins atom 2 on its child class's slots, so a node carrying a slot
;;; the class does not still leaves one unnamed, and `mult-assoc1` mints there.
;;;
;;; Covered by: M3-one-shared-var-two-ops, U1.
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

(rule ((= p (App2 "f3" p1 x q1 y))
       (= dom1 (map-union (map-image p1) (map-image q1)))
       (= mp1 dom1)
       (= avoid1 (map-image mp1))
       (= mx (compose mp1 p1))
       (= csx (ClassSlots x))
       (= my (compose mp1 q1))
       (= csy (ClassSlots y))
       (= csp (ClassSlots p))
       ;; atom 2 -- its root is fresh, so `?x` is the only constraint, and `?z`'s slot is
       ;; named by nothing.  `find-mapping-total` mints there rather than dropping it.
       (= q (App2 "g3" p2 x q2 z))
       (= dom2 (map-union (map-image p2) (map-image q2)))
       (RenamesToLeader x symx x)
       (= mp2 (find-mapping-total avoid1 dom2 (compose (compose mx csx) symx) p2))
       (= im2 (map-image mp2))
       (= avoid2 (map-union avoid1 im2))
       (= mz (compose mp2 q2))
       (= csz (ClassSlots z))
       (= csq (ClassSlots q)))                 ; `?q`'s own narrowing; this action has no use
      ((let rhs (App2 "h3" (compose my csy) y (compose mz csz) z))
       (Equated rhs (compose mp1 csp) p)))


;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
;;; M4.  Two atoms, chained -- the primitive in use.
;;;
;;;     sdql's mult-assoc1:   (* (* ?a ?b) ?c)  =>  (* ?a (* ?b ?c))
;;;
;;; Every flattened nested pattern produces this shape, so it is the one that has to be
;;; cheap, and 38 of sdql's 43 compiled rules are it or a longer chain of it.  It is also
;;; where the per-atom step the rest of the file reuses first appears.  A compiler walks the
;;; atoms in a connected order -- that is compile time, not an order egglog evaluates the
;;; finished body in -- and for atom N it emits:
;;;
;;;     mpN is the least renaming, total on atom N's node slots, agreeing with every
;;;     constraint the atoms before it left -- its root if one of them bound it, every child
;;;     one of them bound, every slot literal one of them pinned.  One `find-mapping-total`,
;;;     avoiding the slots named so far.
;;;
;;; The cases -- first atom / root known / children known -- are only which constraints
;;; happen to exist, not three different rules.  Taking one constraint per case is
;;; under-constrained: for a chain the tempting `(compose p1 symab)` drops an inner slot the
;;; root's edge does not reach, M3's bug again, and `*a` in the tests file is that spelling,
;;; on an e-graph where the two visibly disagree.  What makes an inner slot unreachable is that
;;; the inner NODE carries a slot its class does not -- the root's edge covers the class,
;;; not the node -- so it is real data, not a shape: whether it happens is a property of
;;; the e-graph, and the recipe cannot take the short spelling anywhere.
;;;
;;; Covered by: C2-chain.
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

(rule ((= p (App2 "*" p1 ab q1 c))
       (= dom1 (map-union (map-image p1) (map-image q1)))
       (= mp1 dom1)                            ; atom 1 -- nothing known, so the identity
       (= avoid1 (map-image mp1))              ; slots already spoken for
       (= mab (compose mp1 p1))
       (= csab (ClassSlots ab))
       (= mc (compose mp1 q1))
       (= csc (ClassSlots c))
       (= csp (ClassSlots p))
       ;; atom 2 -- its root `?ab` is the only constraint there is, and that one G-join is
       ;; also the Def. 6 check for `?ab`'s second occurrence
       (= ab (App2 "*" p2 a q2 b))
       (= dom2 (map-union (map-image p2) (map-image q2)))
       (RenamesToLeader ab symab ab)
       (= mp2 (find-mapping-total avoid1 dom2
                                  (compose (compose mab csab) symab)   ; first
                                  (map-domain (compose mab csab))))    ; second: id on its dom
       (= im2 (map-image mp2))
       (= avoid2 (map-union avoid1 im2))
       (= ma (compose mp2 p2))
       (= csa (ClassSlots a))
       (= mb (compose mp2 q2))
       (= csb (ClassSlots b)))
      ((let inner (App2 "*" (compose mb csb) b (compose mc csc) c))
       (let outer (App2 "*" (compose ma csa) a
                            (map-union (map-image (compose mb csb))
                                       (map-image (compose mc csc))) inner))
       (Equated outer (compose mp1 csp) p)))


;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
;;; M5.  The avoid-set, and why it accumulates.
;;;
;;;     ?p == (f5 ?a ?b),  ?a == (g5 ?u ?u),  ?b == (h5 ?v ?v)   =>   ?p = (k5 ?u ?v)
;;;
;;; SYNTHETIC, and for the reason the section is about: the avoid-set matters only when TWO
;;; atoms mint, and an atom mints only for a slot its own node carries and the constraints
;;; do not reach -- a redundant slot (M3, M8).  A redundant slot comes from a MERGE that
;;; narrowed a class below one of its nodes; no rewrite's shape produces one, so no rule can
;;; be quoted whose match is guaranteed to mint twice.  The real rules do mint when the data
;;; hands them a redundant slot, and the shape that most invites it is a root whose two
;;; children share nothing -- the array language's `map-fusion`, `(app (app map ?f) (app
;;; (app map ?g) ?arg))`, whose atoms 2 and 3 are each constrained by their root alone.  It
;;; is compiled in `tests/slotted-array-rules.egg`; its five-node right-hand side is why the
;;; shape is shown here on three small atoms instead.
;;;
;;; `find-mapping-total` is a pure function of its arguments: one call sees one atom's
;;; constraints, and nothing of what another atom invented.  The avoid-set threads that
;;; through as an ordinary value -- a running `map-union` of `(map-image mpN)`, one per atom
;;; the compiler has emitted:
;;;
;;;     (= avoid1 (map-image mp1))                      ; atom 1's slots
;;;     (= mp2    (find-mapping-total avoid1 dom2 ...))  ; atom 2 avoids them
;;;     (= avoid2 (map-union avoid1 (map-image mp2)))    ; accumulate
;;;     (= mp3    (find-mapping-total avoid2 dom3 ...))  ; atom 3 avoids both
;;;
;;; This is S3.6's own condition.  Its matching step requires the union of `m_p` and `m'_p`
;;; to be a bijective renaming, where `m'_p` is the atom's own minimal renaming and `m_p` is
;;; what the atoms before it accumulated -- exactly `avoid` plus `find-mapping`'s injectivity
;;; check.  Identity maps never conflict under `map-union`, so the union is always defined.
;;;
;;; Drop the accumulation and two atoms that both invent pick the same slot, each choosing
;;; the smallest it believes to be free -- here that identifies `?u` with `?v`, so the `k5`
;;; the rule builds has one slot where it should have two.  `f5a` in the tests file is that
;;; rule.  The accumulation is what smallest-unused minting costs: it reuses low names, so
;;; it has to be told which are taken.  `slotted-user-rules.md` records the measurements
;;; behind that policy choice and what the alternative was.
;;;
;;; Covered by: C7-redundant-distinct-vars, which without the accumulation goes order
;;; dependent and loses a match.
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

(rule ((= p (App2 "f5" p1 a q1 b))
       (= dom1 (map-union (map-image p1) (map-image q1)))
       (= mp1 dom1)
       (= avoid1 (map-image mp1))
       (= ma (compose mp1 p1))
       (= csa (ClassSlots a))
       (= mb (compose mp1 q1))
       (= csb (ClassSlots b))
       (= csp (ClassSlots p))
       ;; atom 2 -- chained under `?a`, and minting for whatever that leaves unnamed
       (= a (App2 "g5" p2 u q2 u))
       (= dom2 (map-union (map-image p2) (map-image q2)))
       (RenamesToLeader a syma a)
       (= mp2 (find-mapping-total avoid1 dom2 (compose (compose ma csa) syma)
                                  (map-domain (compose ma csa))))
       (= im2 (map-image mp2))
       (= avoid2 (map-union avoid1 im2))       ; accumulate
       (= mu (compose mp2 p2))
       (= csu (ClassSlots u))
       (RenamesToLeader u symu u)              ; Def. 6 for `?u`'s repeat, as in M2
       (= (compose mp2 q2) (compose (compose mu csu) symu))
       ;; atom 3 -- the same again, and it may not mint over anything atom 2 took
       (= b (App2 "h5" p3 v q3 v))
       (= dom3 (map-union (map-image p3) (map-image q3)))
       (RenamesToLeader b symb b)
       (= mp3 (find-mapping-total avoid2 dom3 (compose (compose mb csb) symb)
                                  (map-domain (compose mb csb))))
       (= im3 (map-image mp3))
       (= avoid3 (map-union avoid2 im3))
       (= mv (compose mp3 p3))
       (= csv (ClassSlots v))
       (RenamesToLeader v symv v)
       (= (compose mp3 q3) (compose (compose mv csv) symv)))
      ((let rhs (App2 "k5" (compose mu csu) u (compose mv csv) v))
       (Equated rhs (compose mp1 csp) p)))


;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
;;; M6.  Two atoms, joined on a fresh root.
;;;
;;;     ?p == (f6 ?x ?y),  ?q == (g6 ?x ?y)   =>   ?p = ?q
;;;
;;; SYNTHETIC, by M3's reason: a fresh root is a multipattern, and no rewrite has one.  This
;;; is the case the reference's `multi_ematch` exists for, and the `f`/`g` are its own test
;;; language's.
;;;
;;; Atom 2's root is fresh, so `mp2` is entirely unknown and every shared variable both
;;; constrains it and contributes part of it: all the pairs go into ONE
;;; `find-mapping-total`.  `symx` and `symy` are free variables, so the join enumerates G(x)
;;; and G(y) -- as it does for `symab` in M4, but several groups at once, and every element
;;; feeds the same call.  M2 is the contrast: there both edges come off one matched row, so
;;; the group element is determined and the `RenamesToLeader` atom is a lookup.
;;;
;;; The tempting demotion is `f6a` in the tests file: solve `mp2` from `?x` alone, giving
;;; `p1 . symx . p2^-1`, and require `?y` to agree with THAT.  That map is defined only on
;;; im(p2), the slots `?x`'s edge reaches, so `(compose mp2 q2)` truncates wherever `?y`'s
;;; edge leaves im(p2): the agreement it asks of `?y` is about a map that does not cover
;;; `?y`'s slots.  One call over all the pairs states a single condition instead, with no
;;; operand partial.  Whether im(p2) happens to cover the whole node is a property of the
;;; data, not of the rule.
;;;
;;; The conclusion is a bare variable, so it is M10's case: two renamed ids, neither at the
;;; identity, solved into one `Equated`.
;;;
;;; Covered by: C3-join.
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

(rule ((= p (App2 "f6" p1 x q1 y))
       (= dom1 (map-union (map-image p1) (map-image q1)))
       (= mp1 dom1)
       (= avoid1 (map-image mp1))
       (= mx (compose mp1 p1))
       (= csx (ClassSlots x))
       (= my (compose mp1 q1))
       (= csy (ClassSlots y))
       (= csp (ClassSlots p))
       ;; atom 2 -- root fresh, so every shared child both constrains mp2 and contributes
       ;; part of it, in one call
       (= q (App2 "g6" p2 x q2 y))
       (= dom2 (map-union (map-image p2) (map-image q2)))
       (RenamesToLeader x symx x)
       (RenamesToLeader y symy y)
       (= mp2 (find-mapping-total avoid1 dom2
                                  (compose (compose mx csx) symx)      ; firsts
                                  (compose (compose my csy) symy)
                                  p2 q2))                              ; seconds
       (= im2 (map-image mp2))
       (= avoid2 (map-union avoid1 im2))
       (= csq (ClassSlots q)))
      ((Equated p (compose (inverse (compose mp1 csp)) (compose mp2 csq)) q)))


;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
;;; M7.  Three atoms -- the cases compose.
;;;
;;;     ?p == (f7 ?a ?b),  ?b == (g7 ?c ?d),  ?q == (h7 ?c ?a)   =>   ?p = ?q
;;;
;;; SYNTHETIC, again because atom 3 is joined on a fresh root.  Three- and four-atom
;;; left-hand sides are ordinary -- the array language's `map-fission` has three, sdql's
;;; `sum-merge` four -- but they are CHAINS, so what they compose is M4 with M4 and M9, and
;;; both are compiled in `tests/slotted-array-rules.egg` and `tests/slotted-sdql-rules.egg`.
;;; What no rewrite composes is a chain with a JOIN, which is this section, so the operators
;;; stay the reference multipattern language's.  (`sum-merge` could not be shown here in any
;;; case: its right-hand side is a six-child `merge`, and the generic encoding stops at
;;; `App4`.)
;;;
;;; Atom 2 is M4's chain, atom 3 is M6's join, and every renaming they produce lives in one
;;; slot space -- the one atom 1 fixed.  Atom 3 is constrained through `?c`, reached via the
;;; chain two atoms deep, and through `?a`, reached straight from atom 1: the case no
;;; two-atom test can exercise.  The avoid-set accumulates across all three (M5).
;;;
;;; Covered by: C10-chain-then-join.
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

(rule ((= p (App2 "f7" p1 a q1 b))
       (= dom1 (map-union (map-image p1) (map-image q1)))
       (= mp1 dom1)
       (= avoid1 (map-image mp1))
       (= ma (compose mp1 p1))
       (= csa (ClassSlots a))
       (= mb (compose mp1 q1))
       (= csb (ClassSlots b))
       (= csp (ClassSlots p))
       ;; atom 2 -- constrained by its root `?b`
       (= b (App2 "g7" p2 c q2 d))
       (= dom2 (map-union (map-image p2) (map-image q2)))
       (RenamesToLeader b symb b)
       (= mp2 (find-mapping-total avoid1 dom2 (compose (compose mb csb) symb)
                                  (map-domain (compose mb csb))))
       (= im2 (map-image mp2))
       (= avoid2 (map-union avoid1 im2))       ; atom 3 may not mint over these
       (= mc (compose mp2 p2))
       (= csc (ClassSlots c))
       (= md (compose mp2 q2))
       (= csd (ClassSlots d))
       ;; atom 3 -- root fresh, constrained through `?c` (depth 2) and `?a` (depth 1)
       (= q (App2 "h7" p3 c q3 a))
       (= dom3 (map-union (map-image p3) (map-image q3)))
       (RenamesToLeader c symc c)
       (RenamesToLeader a syma a)
       (= mp3 (find-mapping-total avoid2 dom3
                                  (compose (compose mc csc) symc)
                                  (compose (compose ma csa) syma)
                                  p3 q3))
       (= im3 (map-image mp3))
       (= avoid3 (map-union avoid2 im3))
       (= csq (ClassSlots q)))
      ((Equated p (compose (inverse (compose mp1 csp)) (compose mp3 csq)) q)))


;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
;;; M8.  A variable wider than its class -- the ClassSlots narrowing.
;;;
;;;     ?x3 == (f8 ?x1 ?x2)   =>   ?x1 = (f8 ?x2 ?x1)
;;;
;;; SYNTHETIC, for M5's reason and not M3's: the narrowing bites only on a node carrying a
;;; slot its class does not, which a merge produces and a rewrite's shape never does.  Every
;;; real rule above is compiled with the narrowing on every variable -- it is one `compose`
;;; per use, unconditional -- but the e-graph that shows it is load-bearing has to be built
;;; by hand, and this rule is shaped to be the smallest one that does.
;;;
;;; The other way to break the edge condition, needing no minting and no chain -- one atom
;;; does it.  A renaming is read off a matched NODE, so its domain is the node's slots, and
;;; Def. 4 permits a node to carry slots its class does not: it requires only `S` included in
;;; `im(m)`, and calls the extras redundant.  An edge has no such slack (M3).  A variable
;;; stands for a CLASS, so writing a renaming read off a node straight into a built node would
;;; name a slot the child does not have.
;;;
;;; The fix is one atom per variable -- `(= csX (ClassSlots X))` -- and `(compose mX csX)`
;;; wherever the renaming is used or written.  A symmetry is NOT a substitute: the self-loop
;;; the machinery stores for a node is the identity on that NODE's slot set, so it can be
;;; exactly as wide as the renaming it was meant to narrow.
;;;
;;; The action's root `?x1` is a child variable and so is the other child `?x2`, so both
;;; renamings come off the matched node and both need narrowing -- and the root's renaming
;;; being a child's is also why the conclusion is `Equated` at `(compose mx1 csx1)` rather
;;; than anything simpler (M10).
;;;
;;; Covered by: C15-action-child-narrowed-to-its-class.  The un-narrowed spelling was the
;;; `wide-kids` mutant, removed from `XDIFF_BUGS` once it stopped discriminating -- the
;;; property is checked directly by `def4-edges.py` instead.  The tests file keeps the
;;; spelling as a counter-example.
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

(rule ((= x3 (App2 "f8" p1 x1 q1 x2))
       (= dom1 (map-union (map-image p1) (map-image q1)))
       (= mp1 dom1)
       (= avoid1 (map-image mp1))
       (= mx1 (compose mp1 p1))
       (= csx1 (ClassSlots x1))
       (= mx2 (compose mp1 q1))
       (= csx2 (ClassSlots x2))
       (= csx3 (ClassSlots x3)))
      ((let rhs (App2 "f8" (compose mx2 csx2) x2 (compose mx1 csx1) x1))
       (Equated rhs (compose mx1 csx1) x1)))


;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
;;; M9.  A slot literal: eta.
;;;
;;;     the array language's eta:   (lam $x (app ?f (var $x)))  =>  ?f
;;;                                  if $x not free in ?f
;;;
;;; Two atoms -- `(var $x)` is not one, because the machinery represents it as an edge to
;;; the `(Var 0)` class, which is also why "the argument is the bound slot" reduces to an
;;; ordinary Def. 6 check on that child.  This is where flattening needs care.  HAZARD:
;;; flattening is not always meaning-preserving.  A nested pattern is matched under one
;;; renaming for the whole pattern, a flattened one gets a renaming per atom, so a slot
;;; written both under a binder and outside it means different things in the two forms and
;;; the binder escapes.  The crate's
;;; `flattening_is_not_faithful_for_a_sibling_slot_literal` is that case; a flattener has to
;;; reject or rename those.
;;;
;;; The side condition is a `map-not-contains` on `?f`'s image -- what "$x is not free in
;;; ?f" becomes once slots are explicit -- and it goes last, after every variable is bound
;;; and every literal pinned.
;;;
;;; Covered by: `eta-fires` and `eta-blocked` in xarray.py -- the same rule, so this section
;;; and `tests/slotted-array-rules.egg` hold the same compiled body -- whose `vac` mode also confirms
;;; the side condition changes the answer on both sides; the same mechanism on binder shapes
;;; is B3, B4 and the `slot-late` mutant, and the slot side condition is CD1-CD4.  xdiff.py's
;;; `atom` form cannot express this shape: two children only, and a slot literal there needs
;;; `Bind` or a unary `Var(Slot)`.
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

(rule ((= e (App2 "lambda" p1 (Var 0) q1 b))
       (= dom1 (map-union (map-image p1) (map-image q1)))
       (= mp1 dom1)
       (= avoid1 (map-image mp1))
       (= sx (map-get (compose mp1 p1) 0))     ; the bound slot, in pattern slots
       (= mb (compose mp1 q1))
       (= csb (ClassSlots b))
       (= cse (ClassSlots e))
       ;; atom 2 -- constrained by its root `?b` AND by the slot literal `$x`, which atom 1
       ;; pinned to `sx`.  The pinning has to be one of `find-mapping-total`'s constraints --
       ;; `mp2 . q2 = {0 -> sx}` -- and not a test on its result: a call that does not carry
       ;; it is free to mint a fresh name for that slot, and `mp2` is then a map no later
       ;; equality can repair.  That is the `slot-late` mutant.
       (= b (App2 "app" p2 f q2 (Var 0)))
       (= dom2 (map-union (map-image p2) (map-image q2)))
       (RenamesToLeader b symb b)
       (= mp2 (find-mapping-total avoid1 dom2
                                  (compose (compose mb csb) symb)
                                  (map-insert (map-empty) 0 sx)
                                  (map-domain (compose mb csb))
                                  q2))
       (= im2 (map-image mp2))
       (= avoid2 (map-union avoid1 im2))
       (= sx (map-get (compose mp2 q2) 0))     ; the literal's second use: a constraint
       (= mf (compose mp2 p2))
       (= csf (ClassSlots f))
       (map-not-contains (map-image (compose mf csf)) sx))   ; $x not free in ?f
      ((Equated e (compose (inverse (compose mp1 cse)) (compose mf csf)) f)))


;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
;;; M10.  Actions: concluding over renamed ids.
;;;
;;;     sdql's add-zero:   (+ ?e 0)  =>  ?e
;;;
;;; The paper's union takes renamed ids -- S3.5 is union(m1*a1, m2*a2) -- and which of
;;; redundancy, a new group element, or an e-class merge it produces depends on those
;;; renamings.  egglog's `union` takes e-classes, i.e. only the case where both renamings
;;; are the identity, so an action cannot use it: `?e`'s renaming into pattern slots is
;;; whatever the `+` node's edge was, and the root's is whatever `ClassSlots` left, and the
;;; compiler does not know either is the identity.  So the action always writes the fact
;;; itself, with the two renamings solved into one:
;;;
;;;     from  mr * Root = ma * A   follows   Root = (mr^-1 . ma) * A
;;;
;;; and `(Equated Root (compose (inverse mr) ma) A)` is that.  Concluding `(union Root A)`
;;; instead is the encoder's `union-id` mutant, and it is not a shortcut but a different
;;; claim -- it asserts the two agree at the identity, which collapses `ClassSlots` to the
;;; intersection of two slot sets that were never meant to be compared.  It shows up as
;;; spurious redundancy; the tests file's `+a` is it.
;;;
;;; `Equated` is the unoriented form of `RenamesToLeader` and is what a compiler should
;;; write: an action cannot know which of the two values is the smaller, and the machinery
;;; picks the side.
;;;
;;; HAZARD: do not write `RenamesToLeader` from an action.  It carries an orientation -- the
;;; leader is the smaller of the pair -- and nothing re-orients it: a row that gets it wrong
;;; is deleted as stale, so the fact is silently lost, and transitivity and single parent can
;;; then disagree forever.
;;;
;;; The `0` also shows how a ground leaf sits in a child column: not written into the column,
;;; but reached through its class.  Two values can be slotted-equal without being egglog-
;;; equal, so the class holding `(Num 0)` need not have that row in the column, and the atom
;;; joins `(RenamesToLeader (Num 0) mzero zero)` to get a handle that does match.
;;;
;;; Covered by: C11, C14, E1-E3.
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

(rule ((= e (App2 "+" p1 x q1 zero))
       (RenamesToLeader (Num 0) mzero zero)    ; the literal, through its class
       (= dom1 (map-union (map-image p1) (map-image q1)))
       (= mp1 dom1)
       (= avoid1 (map-image mp1))
       (= mx (compose mp1 p1))
       (= csx (ClassSlots x))
       (= cse (ClassSlots e)))
      ((Equated e (compose (inverse (compose mp1 cse)) (compose mx csx)) x)))


;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
;;; M11.  A nested right-hand side.
;;;
;;;     the array language's let-app:
;;;         (let $x (app ?a ?b) ?e)  =>  (app (let $x ?a ?e) (let $x ?b ?e))
;;;             if $x free in ?a or in ?b
;;;
;;; The right-hand side is a *tree*, so an intermediate node must exist before the outer one
;;; can point at it: one `let` per built node, innermost first.  Two of them here, since the
;;; rule duplicates the substitution.
;;;
;;; WHICH RENAMINGS AN ACTION USES, since this is the part that looks like it needs a choice
;;; and does not.  An action never solves a renaming.  It is written in PATTERN slot space,
;;; and every renaming it needs already exists:
;;;
;;;   * a child that is a pattern variable `x` takes the pair `(mx, x)` -- the renaming the
;;;     LHS already computed for that variable, and its leader, narrowed by `(ClassSlots x)`
;;;     as M8 requires.  Nothing is recomputed here.
;;;   * a child that is another built node takes the IDENTITY on that node's own slots.  Both
;;;     nodes are already in pattern slot space, so there is no renaming between them.
;;;   * a built node's slots are the union of its edges' images, MINUS what it binds -- the
;;;     `map-remove` below, since the node carries the bound slot and its class does not.
;;;     That is what forces the walk to be bottom-up: the inner node's edges must exist
;;;     before the outer node's edge to it can be written.
;;;   * a slot literal is an edge `{0 -> sx}` to `(Var 0)`, whether it is a binder column or
;;;     an occurrence.
;;;
;;; So `find-mapping-total` never appears in an action.  It appears only INSIDE an `mx`, and
;;; only when `x` was bound by an atom whose renaming had to be solved -- atom 2 onward.
;;; Atom 1's `mp1` is the identity on the pattern's slots, so a variable it bound contributes
;;; its stored edge essentially unchanged (`?e` here), while a variable atom 2 bound
;;; contributes `(compose mp2 <col>)` with `mp2` from `find-mapping-total` (`?a`, `?b`).  The
;;; action does not distinguish the two: it writes `mx` either way, and the LHS decided what
;;; that is.
;;;
;;; The conclusion needs one renaming that is not a child's: the built node is in pattern
;;; slots and the matched root is in its own, so the edge between them is the root's renaming
;;; into pattern slots -- `(compose mp1 csp)` here.  That is the only renaming in an action
;;; that is about the ROOT rather than a child, which is why M10 treats concluding as its own
;;; step.
;;;
;;; Covered by: NR1-nested-build, NR2, NR3-assoc, and `let-app-fires` / `let-app-blocked`
;;; in xarray.py -- again the same rule, so this section and `tests/slotted-array-rules.egg`
;;; hold the same compiled body.
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

(rule ((= p (App3 "let" p1 (Var 0) q1 ab r1 e))
       (= dom1 (map-union (map-image p1) (map-union (map-image q1) (map-image r1))))
       (= mp1 dom1)
       (= avoid1 (map-image mp1))
       (= sx (map-get (compose mp1 p1) 0))
       (= mab (compose mp1 q1))
       (= csab (ClassSlots ab))
       (= me (compose mp1 r1))
       (= cse (ClassSlots e))
       (= csp (ClassSlots p))
       ;; atom 2 -- the chain again
       (= ab (App2 "app" p2 a q2 b))
       (= dom2 (map-union (map-image p2) (map-image q2)))
       (RenamesToLeader ab symab ab)
       (= mp2 (find-mapping-total avoid1 dom2 (compose (compose mab csab) symab)
                                  (map-domain (compose mab csab))))
       (= im2 (map-image mp2))
       (= avoid2 (map-union avoid1 im2))
       (= ma (compose mp2 p2))
       (= csa (ClassSlots a))
       (= mb (compose mp2 q2))
       (= csb (ClassSlots b))
       ;; the side condition: `$x` free in `?a` or in `?b`.  A disjunction cannot be a fact,
       ;; so it is a value and `guard` reads it.
       (guard (or (bool-map-contains (map-image (compose ma csa)) sx)
                  (bool-map-contains (map-image (compose mb csb)) sx))))
      ;; innermost first: the two `let`s, then the `app` over them
      ((let leta (App3 "let" (map-insert (map-empty) 0 sx) (Var 0)
                             (compose ma csa) a (compose me cse) e))
       (let letb (App3 "let" (map-insert (map-empty) 0 sx) (Var 0)
                             (compose mb csb) b (compose me cse) e))
       (let rhs (App2 "app"
                      (map-remove (map-union (map-image (map-insert (map-empty) 0 sx))
                                             (map-union (map-image (compose ma csa))
                                                        (map-image (compose me cse)))) sx)
                      leta
                      (map-remove (map-union (map-image (map-insert (map-empty) 0 sx))
                                             (map-union (map-image (compose mb csb))
                                                        (map-image (compose me cse)))) sx)
                      letb))
       (Equated rhs (compose mp1 csp) p)))
